  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 516 PM EDT FRI AUG 14 2026\n\nWe will start the period still at the southern edge of faster \nmid-upper level flow (slightly south of due westerly), with ridging \naloft over the Deep South. With dry mid levels expected and a lack \nof discernible significant features to focus convection, models are \ngenerating little or no precip for our area on Sunday. Have gone \nwith a dry forecast as given by the NBM, but based on forecast \nsoundings, wouldn't be surprised if at least some isolated activity \noccurred in our northeast counties.\n\nA ripple moving through the mid-upper level flow to our north will \nsupport a weak cold front which is expected to approach us Sunday \nnight and move through on Monday or Monday evening. Deeper moisture \nshould return ahead of the front, with our PWs rising to around 2\" \nagain. With this, the potential for thunderstorms returns. Drier air \narriving behind the front will probably keep us dry on Tuesday. \nHowever, there is a shortwave trough showing up in the GFS which \nmoves through and results in a bit of precip. The required NBM \nforecast is dry on Tuesday with the exception of some 20% POP near \nthe TN and VA border. If the shortwave and its precip persist in \nlater runs, a low POP could end up over more of the area.\n\nThe next significant shot at precip arrives in the neighborhood of \nWednesday night and Thursday as an upper level impulse carves out an \neastern CONUS trough. This sends another cold front southeast, with \natmospheric moisture again increasing ahead of the front. Behind the \nfront, there could be some noticeable relief from the mugginess at \nthe end of the week.",
